"""Written chapter introductions and practice for Learn with Mynul."""

# title, source lesson range, introduction, rule, worked example, line-by-line trace
CHAPTERS = [
('Your first Python program',1,3,
 'A program is a set of instructions. Python executes the statements at the top level of a file in order. You write a .py file, save it and ask the Python interpreter to run it. A notebook uses the same language but keeps results between cell runs.',
 'On Windows, install Python from python.org. Open a terminal and check py --version. Save print("Hello, Mynul!") in hello.py and run py hello.py. On macOS or Linux the command is usually python3 hello.py. Do not put the terminal command inside the Python file. In a notebook, restart the kernel and run all cells before trusting the result.',
 'import math  # Load the standard-library math module.\nname = "Mynul"  # Bind a name to a string.\nprint("Hello", name)  # Print two values separated by a space.\nprint(math.sqrt(81))  # Call sqrt and print its result.',
 'Import makes math available. Assignment binds name to "Mynul". The first print produces Hello Mynul. sqrt(81) returns the float 9.0. A syntax error stops a file before its statements run; a runtime error happens while an instruction is executing.'),
('Variables and numeric types',4,6,
 'A variable is a name referring to a Python object. Assignment binds or rebinds that name. It does not create a permanent box restricted to one type. Start with int for whole numbers, float for approximate decimal values and bool for True or False.',
 'Use type(value) to inspect a type. Division / returns a float for integer operands. // performs floor division. Empty containers, empty strings, zero and None are falsy. A nonempty string such as "False" is truthy. Floating-point arithmetic is approximate; use math.isclose for approximate comparisons.',
 'count = 3  # int\nprice = 2.5  # float\ntotal = count * price  # 7.5\nprint(total)\nprint(bool("False"))  # A nonempty string is truthy.\nprint(type(count).__name__)',
 'count refers to 3. Multiplication produces 7.5 and binds total to it. The output is 7.5, True and int on separate lines. Reassigning count = "three" would change count to refer to a string, without changing total.'),
('Strings, slicing and casting',7,10,
 'A string is an immutable sequence of Unicode characters. Indexing retrieves one character. Slicing builds a new string. This is useful for cleaning user input, extracting a filename or preparing text for an AI model.',
 'Indexes start at 0. -1 means the last character. In text[start:stop:step], stop is excluded. A slice may run past the end safely, but a single invalid index raises IndexError. input() returns a string, so convert it before doing numeric arithmetic.',
 'text = "PYTHON"\nprint(text[0])  # P\nprint(text[-1])  # N\nprint(text[1:4])  # YTH, excluding index 4\nprint(text[::-1])  # Reverse into a new string.\nage = int("31")  # Convert numeric text to an integer.\nprint(age + 1)',
 'The positive indexes are 0 through 5. The slice starts at index 1 and stops before index 4. A step of -1 walks backward. int("31") produces 31 so addition produces 32. int("3.5") raises ValueError because that text is not an integer literal.'),
('Operators and formatted output',11,16,
 'Operators combine values, compare objects or update names. Before using a long expression, identify the operands and predict the result type. Parentheses make your intention easier to read.',
 '== compares values. is compares object identity. Use is None when checking for None, but use == for ordinary numeric or string equality. and and or short-circuit and return an operand. An f-string inserts values into text using braces. Use :.2f to display two decimal places.',
 'a = [1, 2]\nb = a.copy()  # A new list containing equal values.\nprint(a == b)  # True: equal contents.\nprint(a is b)  # False: different objects.\nprice = 12.5\nprint(f"Total: {price:.2f} EUR")',
 'copy creates a separate list. Equality is True because the values match. Identity is False because these are separate objects. The final line prints Total: 12.50 EUR. Formatting changes the displayed text, not the stored float.'),
('Decisions with if, elif and else',17,20,
 'A conditional selects a block of instructions. The condition is evaluated first. Python then executes the first matching branch and skips the other branches of that same if statement.',
 'End an if, elif or else header with a colon. Indent each body consistently, normally four spaces. Put specific conditions before broader ones. A second independent if is a separate decision, while elif belongs to the preceding decision.',
 'score = 75\nif score >= 80:\n    print("Excellent")\nelif score >= 50:\n    print("Pass")\nelse:\n    print("Try again")',
 '75 >= 80 is False, so the first body is skipped. 75 >= 50 is True, so Pass prints. Python skips else. If score were 90, only Excellent would print, even though 90 also satisfies the second comparison.'),
('Loops and control flow',21,25,
 'A loop repeats a block. A for loop takes values from an iterable. A while loop repeats while a condition remains truthy. You need both when processing files, records or batches of training data.',
 'range(start, stop, step) excludes stop. break exits the nearest loop. continue skips the rest of the current iteration. pass is a placeholder that does nothing. In a while loop, ensure the condition can eventually become false. A nested loop finishes its inner iterations for each outer value.',
 'total = 0\nfor number in range(1, 5):  # 1, 2, 3, 4\n    if number == 3:\n        continue  # Skip adding 3.\n    total += number\nprint(total)',
 'Start at 0. Add 1 to get 1, then add 2 to get 3. At 3, continue jumps to the next iteration. Add 4 to get 7. The output is 7. range never yields 5.'),
('Lists, copying and comprehensions',26,29,
 'A list is an ordered mutable collection. It is useful for storing rows, predictions or a sequence of tasks. A list can contain different types, but a consistent structure makes real programs easier to maintain.',
 'append changes a list in place and returns None. Assignment such as b = a gives another name to the same list. a.copy() makes a shallow copy: nested objects are still shared. A list comprehension builds a new list by transforming and optionally filtering items.',
 'values = [1, 2, 3]\nalias = values  # Both names refer to the same list.\nalias.append(4)\nsquares = [x * x for x in values if x % 2 == 0]\nprint(values)\nprint(squares)',
 'append changes the shared list to [1, 2, 3, 4]. The comprehension keeps 2 and 4 because their remainders modulo 2 are zero. Squaring them produces [4, 16]. values stays as [1, 2, 3, 4].'),
('Tuples and sets',30,33,
 'Choose a collection based on what you need to do. A tuple is an ordered immutable sequence, often used for a fixed record. A set stores unique hashable elements and is useful for deduplicating data and checking membership.',
 'A one-item tuple needs a trailing comma: (5,). {} creates an empty dictionary; set() creates an empty set. Set order is not a contract, so sort a set before displaying predictable output. Intersection keeps shared elements; union keeps elements from either set.',
 'point = (3, 4)\nx, y = point  # Unpack two values into two names.\na = {"python", "sql"}\nb = {"python", "linux"}\nprint(sorted(a & b))\nprint(len(a | b))',
 'Unpacking binds x to 3 and y to 4. Only python appears in both sets, so the first output is [\'python\']. The union has python, sql and linux, so its length is 3. Sorting is only for stable display.'),
('Dictionaries and text cleaning',34,36,
 'A dictionary maps unique hashable keys to values. Use it for structured records, lookup tables and frequency counters. String methods help turn messy input into consistent dictionary keys.',
 'record[key] raises KeyError if a key is missing. record.get(key, default) returns a fallback. Iterating a dictionary yields keys; .items() yields key-value pairs. strip removes characters from the ends and lower returns lowercase text. Strings are immutable, so keep the returned result.',
 'words = " Python SQL python ".strip().lower().split()\ncounts = {}\nfor word in words:\n    counts[word] = counts.get(word, 0) + 1\nprint(counts["python"])\nprint(counts.get("java", 0))',
 'Cleaning gives the words python, sql and python. The first occurrence starts from zero and adds one. The second python increments the existing count to 2. Looking up missing java with get returns 0 without adding a new key.'),
('Functions, arguments and scope',37,41,
 'A function packages reusable work behind a name. Parameters are local names that receive arguments. Calling a function starts its body; defining a function does not run that body.',
 'return sends a result to the caller and ends the function. print displays text and returns None. A function that reaches its end without return also returns None. Defaults are evaluated once when the function is defined; use None instead of a mutable default such as []. *args collects extra positional arguments and **kwargs collects extra keyword arguments.',
 'def average(values):\n    if not values:\n        return None  # Avoid division by zero.\n    return sum(values) / len(values)\n\nresult = average([2, 4, 6])\nprint(result)\nprint(average([]))',
 'The definition creates the function. The call binds values to [2, 4, 6]. sum is 12 and len is 3, so return sends back 4.0. The empty list takes the early return and produces None. values is local to each function call.'),
('Functions as values, lambda and map',42,44,
 'A function is an object. You can store it in a variable, pass it into another function or return it. This is how callback functions and configurable data transformations work.',
 'A lambda is a small anonymous function containing one expression. map applies a function to each item and returns an iterator. filter keeps items for which a function returns a truthy value. reduce combines items into one result and must be imported from functools. A comprehension is often easier to read.',
 'double = lambda x: x * 2\nvalues = [1, 2, 3]\nprint(list(map(double, values)))\nprint(list(filter(lambda x: x > 1, values)))\nprint([double(x) for x in values])',
 'map calls double on 1, 2 and 3, producing 2, 4 and 6. list consumes the iterator for display. filter keeps 2 and 3. The comprehension produces the same values as map. In Python 3, a consumed iterator does not automatically restart.'),
('Exceptions and reliable input',45,45,
 'An exception signals that a normal operation could not finish. Handling an expected error lets a program provide a useful response. Unexpected errors should remain visible so you can fix the cause.',
 'Put the risky operation in try. Catch a specific exception such as ValueError. else runs if try completed without an exception. finally runs when leaving the statement, including during most exception propagation. Avoid except: pass because it hides useful information.',
 'text = "twelve"\ntry:\n    age = int(text)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Please enter a whole number.")\nelse:\n    print(age + 1)\nfinally:\n    print("Finished checking.")',
 'int("twelve") raises ValueError. Assignment to age does not finish. The matching handler prints the request for a whole number. else is skipped. finally prints Finished checking. The program continues after the try statement.'),
('Classes and objects',46,46,
 'A class defines the behavior and structure of a kind of object. An instance is one particular object created from that class. Use classes when data and the operations on it belong together.',
 'self refers to the instance receiving a method call. __init__ initializes instance state. Attributes such as self.balance belong to that instance. Class attributes are shared through the class, so do not use a mutable class attribute for data that should be separate for every instance. Inheritance reuses behavior; composition stores another object as part of an object.',
 'class Counter:\n    def __init__(self, start=0):\n        self.value = start\n\n    def increment(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self.value\n\na = Counter()\nb = Counter(10)\nprint(a.increment())\nprint(b.value)',
 'Creating a calls __init__ with start=0. Creating b uses start=10. increment changes only a.value and returns 1. b.value is still 10 because the two instances have separate state.'),
('AI concepts from prompts to training',0,0,
 'Python is the programming foundation. AI vocabulary helps you understand what you are building. Read the terms below, then practice distinguishing model training, inference and retrieval. This chapter explains concepts; it does not train a large language model.',
 'An LLM generates tokens using learned patterns and the available context. Fluent output can still be wrong. Temperature changes the sampling distribution and does not guarantee factual accuracy. Retrieval adds selected source material at inference time; fine-tuning changes model weights through additional training.',
 'documents = {\n    "hours": "The library closes at 18:00.",\n    "loans": "Books can be borrowed for 14 days.",\n}\nquestion = "When does the library close?"\ncontext = documents["hours"]  # Retrieval chooses relevant evidence.\nprompt = f"Answer using this source: {context}\\nQuestion: {question}"\nprint(prompt)  # This builds input; it does not call an AI model.',
 'The document is evidence, the question is the request and the prompt assembles both. A model would still need to be called separately. A production retrieval system must select sources, handle missing evidence and evaluate its answers. API secrets belong on a server, never in a public GitHub Pages file.'),
]

def drill(prompt, answer, hint, code='', why=''):
    return dict(prompt=prompt, accept=[answer], hint=hint, code=code, answer=answer, why=why or hint)

